	<key>readme</key>
	<string>## Alfred LAPS

Look up Windows LAPS and legacy Microsoft LAPS passwords in Active Directory without leaving Alfred. The workflow uses the bundled [laps-cli](https://github.com/jankkm/laps-cli).

### Setup

1. Set your **AD Domain** and **User** in *Configure Workflow…*.
2. Type `laps`. On first use, Alfred asks for your AD password, tests it against Active Directory, and saves it in your **macOS Keychain** (item "Alfred LAPS"). The password is never written to the workflow configuration or passed on a command line.

### Usage

- `laps PC123` looks up a computer. Separate several names with commas or spaces: `laps PC1, PC2, PC3`.
- Select a hostname anywhere, then use the Universal Action **Get LAPS Password**.
- Leave the field empty to see your recent lookups. Recent lookups store names only, never passwords.

| Key | Action |
|---|---|
| ↩ | Look up the typed name. Once results are shown, ↩ copies the selected password |
| ⌘↩ | Paste the password into the frontmost app |
| ⌘T | Auto-type the password into the frontmost app, for VM consoles, RDP or KVM where paste doesn't work |
| ⌘L / ⌥↩ | Show in Large Type |
| ⌘R | Look up again |
| ⌘, | Change or forget your saved AD password |
| ↑ ↓ | Move between results |

Copied passwords are marked *transient*, so they never appear in Alfred's Clipboard History. They can also be cleared from the clipboard automatically (see *Clear Clipboard*).

### Result details

Each result shows the managed account, the LAPS flavour (Windows LAPS encrypted or cleartext, or legacy LAPS), when the password rotates, and when it was last set. Passwords expiring within a day are flagged in orange.

### Auto-type

⌘T types the password keystroke by keystroke into whatever app is in front once Alfred closes. Alfred needs **Accessibility** permission (System Settings › Privacy &amp; Security › Accessibility). You can set the typing speed, and whether to press Return afterwards, in *Configure Workflow*.
</string>
